{
  "project": {
    "name": "Libro MCP",
    "productDescription": "Reusable MCP for verifiable human identity, signed publications, and publishing by human-authorized agents.",
    "productSummary": "## Libro MCP\n\nConnect an MCP client to https://libro-mcp.vercel.app/mcp to use Libro in your own application, publishing workflow, or agent.\n\n### Identity and author profiles\n\nUse World ID-backed identity and author handles to associate work with a verified human. Read public author profiles and manage your profile through authorized tools.\n\n### Human publishing\n\nPublish documents with a human authorship proof bound to the exact signed content. A writing tool or website can submit a publication and direct its author through browser verification and signing.\n\n### Human-authorized agents\n\nAuthorize an agent to publish on your behalf, inspect its registrations, and revoke its authority. Agent signatures identify human-authorized work as a distinct proof class from direct human authorship.\n\n### Reading and verification\n\nBuild readers, archives, and verification tools using public canonical publications and author lookups. Retrieve complete signed payloads and verify their registrations on World Chain.",
    "technicalDescription": "Headless Next.js MCP with dedicated Postgres storage, World ID proofs, and World Chain publication registrations.",
    "technicalSummary": "## Connect and reuse\n\nProduction MCP endpoint: https://libro-mcp.vercel.app/mcp. Use an MCP client to initialize the connection and discover tools. Public author and publication reads require no login. Protected tools enforce OAuth identity, scopes, and signing authorization; follow the advertised authorization metadata and browser signing links.\n\n## Implementation\n\nLibro MCP exposes its application interface at /mcp. It owns canonical identities and publications, OAuth state, prepared signing operations, chain verification, a relayer, and a durable event outbox. Browser login, standard OAuth HTTP endpoints, and signing screens are hosted by a separate web client. World ID proofs bind human identity and exact publication signals; human-authorized agents use EIP-712 signatures. Registration verification queries configured World Chain RPC endpoints in parallel.\n\n## System and sources\n\nThe system view covers identity, publication, verification, canonical storage, external clients, and chain dependencies across logical, technical, and provider layers. The verified source inventory may include applications outside this boundary.\n\n## Deployment\n\nUse Node.js 24 and pnpm 10.34.5. Install with pnpm install --frozen-lockfile, configure a dedicated Postgres database and required environment variables, apply pnpm --filter @libro/service db:migrate, then build with pnpm --filter @libro/service build. See libro/service/README.md for deployment and authorization configuration. The published production endpoint is reusable; provider regions, database bindings, and live health are not asserted by the system model."
  },
